In the valley where the sun forgets to set, there lived a baker named Liora who sang with her sourdough. Every morning she kneaded flour into bread that carried the memory of yesterday. The villagers came not for the crust, but for the patience baked into the crumb. For in that bread, a story could bloom, and once it bloomed, the eater would understand a truth they hadn’t known they sought.

One dusk, a thin deer with eyes like polished glass wandered into the oven’s warmth. It spoke without a mouth, in a language woven from wind and wheat. “Baker,” it said, “you give your loaves thought and sweat. I give you the power to listen.” Liora pressed a loaf into the deer’s silence and waited. The deer tasted, then vanished into a shower of sesame seeds that drifted across the village like tiny suns. When the seeds settled, every person who tasted the bread could hear the soft, unspoken needs of others—the ache in a neighbor’s back, the quiet longing of a shy child, the tiredness of the night watchman.

From that day, the village traded more than bread. They traded attention. They learned to listen as if listening could bend time, and in bending time, their small world grew large enough to hold one more dream. The oven glowed with a patient star across every season, reminding them that nourishment is not only a meal but a listening heart.
